#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
fConfig(){ :;

	## Defaults
	default_outBase="62"
	default_outFormat="%d" #............: A string with possible variables: %d[ate], %h[ost], %f[QDN], %m[AC], %u[UID], %r[andom]
	default_dtAlgo=1
	default_precision_DateTime=0 #...: 0, -1, 1
	dofault_doLCaseIfAppropriate=1
	default_doHash_UserName=1
	default_doHash_HostName=1
	default_doHash_FQDN=1
	default_keepOnlyRighmostHashChars_UserName=5
	default_keepOnlyRighmostHashChars_HostName=5
	default_keepOnlyRighmostHashChars_FQDN=8
	default_randomCharCount=4
	default_doClipOut=0

}


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
fPrint_AboutAndSyntax(){
	((doQuietly)) && return
	fEcho_Clean ""
	#  X-------------------------------------------------------------------------------X
	cat <<- EOF_ng50ne2
		Multiversal Unique ID. Can be way more unique than UUID, but by default, is
		less unique - but also much shorter, human-readable/sortable, while still
		privacy-protecting. By default, it converts the current time using --dtalgo 1
		below, transformed to a base ${default_outBase} string.

		Syntax: ${meName} [optional args]
		  Optional arguments:

		    --format <FORMAT> (default if not specified: "%d")
		      Any string that may contain the following the following variables:
		        %datetime ...: Date/time (format according to --dtalgo)
		        %username ...: Username, by default cryptographically hashed
		        %hostname ...: Hostname, by default cryptographically hashed
		        %fqdn .......: Fully-qualified host and domain name
		        %mac ........: MAC address of the first network adapter with a gateway
		        %guid .......: UUID v4 (%uuid also acceptable)
		        %random .....: Randomly-generated number
		      You can include any other text, eg delimiters. You may also use common
		      abbreviations for each (eg "%dt" instead of "%datetime"), or just the
		      first letter in unambiguous cases.

		    --base <base>: The output base (${default_outBase} if not specified), one of:
		      Actual standards:
		         10 ....: aka Decimal [default] ...... 0-9
		         16 ....: aka Hexedecimal ............ 0-9, A-F
		         32[r]..: RFC 4648 ................... A-Z, 2-7
		         32h ...: RFC 4648 §7, 'Base32Hex' ... 0-9, A-V
		         32w ...: Wordsafe Base32 ............ 2-9, CFGHJMPQRVWX, cfghjmpqrvwx
		         36 ....: Just alphanum .............. 0-9, A-Z
		         62 ....: All alphanum ............... 0-9, A-Z, a-z
		         64[r]..: RFC 4648 ................... 0-9, A-Z, a-z, +, /
		         64u ...: RFC 4648 §5; URL-safe ...... 0-9, A-Z, a-z, -, _
		      De-facto standards:
		         26 ....: One-case alphabetic ........ A-Z
		         32c ...: Crockford's Base32 ......... 0-9, A-Z no I, L, O, U
		         52 ....: Both-case alphabetic ....... A-Z, a-z
		      Very slightly custom:
		         64j1u..: Slight alternate to 64u but unicode instead of "-_"
		      Fully custom (URL-safe, filesystem-safe, word-safe, programmer-friendly):
		         48j1...: 0-9, cfghjmpqrvwx, and lots of unicode symbols
		         64j1uw.: 0-9, Like 48j1 but with upper-case too
		        128[j1].: Like 64j1u but with way more unicode symbols
		        256[j1].: 0-9, A-Z, a-z, and lots of unicode chars
		      Recommendations:
		        ▸ Max long-term standardization .....: 26, 36, 52, 62
		        ▸ Balance standard w/ word-safety ...: 32c, 32w
		        ▸ Standard, URL-safe, filesystem-safe: 26, 32*, 36, 52, 62, 64u
		        ▸ URL & FS-safe, programmer-friendly : 26, 32*, 36, 52, 62, *j1*
		        ▸ Max word-safety ...................: 32w, *j1* except 64j1u

		    --dtalgo <TYPE> (default if not specified: 1)
		      1: Converts current UTC unix seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00, to a
		         shorter serial string. Pros: ▸Tied for shortest. Cons: ▸While results
		         are self-comparable, the least human-readable among these. ▸Won't
		         serialize dates prior to 1970 (to avoid extra symbols for negative).
		      2: Treats current UTC time in YYYYMMDDHHmmSS as one big integer, then
		         converts it to the specified base. Pros: ▸Simplest. Cons:
		         ▸Least efficient. ▸While results are self-comparable, the second-least
		         human-readable.
		      3: Treats [YYYY][MM][DD][HH][mm][SS] as separate fields, base-converts
		         each separately, then rejoins into one string. Pros: ▸Fixed-position
		         fields. ▸Can be human-readable. ▸Longest option for bases <62 (eg 32).
		      4: Like #2 for [YYYY][MM][DD], but each day ([HH][mm][SS]) is divided up
		         evenly to maximize the granularity of the entire specified numeric
		         base. Pros: ▸Shortest. ▸Fixed-position & human-comparable to the day.
		         ▸With practice, the short time-of-day field is arguably even useful.

		    --dtprecision <-1|0|1> The millenium and/or second precision for date/time:
		      ▸  0 : Default; eg millenium & 1 second depending on --dtalgo and --base
		      ▸ -1 : Less precise, OK to year 2999, ~minute-level precision
		      ▸  1 : More precise, millisecond depending on --dtalgo and --base

		    --random-char-count: If using %r, how many characters
		    --lcase <0|1>......: Convert to lcase for single-case bases (default: ${dofault_doLCaseIfAppropriate})
		    --clipout <0|1>....: Copy output to clipboard (default: ${default_doClipOut})
		    --help ............: Show extra information on options and bases.

		    --hash-[username|hostname|fqdn] <0|1>:
		      If %h|%f are in --format, sha256 the hostname or FQDN

		    --keep-only-righmost-hash-chars-[username|hostname|fqdn] <NUM>
		      If --hash-[username|hostname|fqdn] is in effect, keep only the righntmost
		      NUM chars of the resulting hash

		    --dtoverride <"date/time string recognizable by 'date' command">
		      Instead of using the current UTC date/time, use the specified date/time.
		      Be sure to provide enough precision for for other arguments, e.g.
		      "2025-06-21 15:45:31.951" if also passing '--dtprecision 1'.
	EOF_ng50ne2
	#  X-------------------------------------------------------------------------------X
	fEcho_Force ""
}


#•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
fPrint_Help(){
	((doQuietly)) && return
	fEcho_Clean ""
	#  X-------------------------------------------------------------------------------X
	cat <<- EOF_ng50mzv
		Examples of --dtalgo, for a UTC date of 2025-06-21 at 3:45:31 PM:

		--dtalgo     base10    base62    Res²  #³  Till⁴  prec -1⁵  #  Res   Till
		  1      1750520731    1uT0PL     1s   6   3769     1yPwr   5   1m   3711
		  2  20250621154531  5kWSUchH     1s   8  21834    RM656b   6   1m   2056
		  3  20250621154531   Wf6LFjV     1s   7   3843     P6LFj   5   1m   2061
		  4   2025062106566   Wf6L1hu    >1s   7   3843     P6Lei   5  22s   2061

		--dtalgo              base32c¹   Res   #   Till   prec -1   #  Res   Till
		  1                   1m5dmwv     1m   7   3058     vtbhh   5   1m   2033
		  2                 jdbvh9573     1m   9   3519   2ap3g6s   7   1m   2343
		  3                1z96nf1d0z     1m  10  32768    s6nf1d   6   1m   2031
		  4                  1z96nn0c   2.6s   8  32768   1z96nn0   7  84s  32768

		Footnotes:

		  ¹: Listed here is Crockford base32 variant with --lcase.
		  ²: Time resolution; the number of seconds per unique rightmost character.
		  ³: Character count in the output.
		  ⁴: The year that the character count will necessarily increase by 1.
		  ⁵: If '--dtprecision -1' flag used, eg no millenium and/or seconds.

		Notes:

		  ▸ For any given use-case, you should never mix type, base, and/or precision,
		    or the very purpose for using this tool could be obviated and you'd wind up
		    hating life at best, or with data collisions and/or loss data at worst. If
		    you might do so, you should prepend the type, base and precision to the
		    result (which is longer than just "YYYYMMDDHHMMSS" in decimal form), or at
		    least note them in another field.

		  ▸ The following bases are resistant to accidentally spelling out naughty
		    words: 32c[rockford], and especially 32w, 48j1, 64j1uw, and 128j1.

		  ▸ All --dtalgos and bases are human-readable/comparable. But not even RFC
		    standard bases >36 will machine-sort properly with common sort regimes, eg
		    LC_COLLATE=C.UTF-8, etc.

		Recommendations for various --dtalgo goals:

		  ▸ Human parseability and brevity ................: --dtalgo 4 --base 62
		  ▸ Absolute brevity and URL/file/programmer safety: --dtalgo 1 --base 256j1
		  ▸ Maximum readability with bad handwriting ......:            --base 32c
		  ▸ Minimal odds of accidental bad words ..........:            --base 32w
		  ▸ Human parseability of time fields .............: --dtalgo 3
	EOF_ng50mzv
	#  X-------------------------------------------------------------------------------X
	fEcho_Force ""
}


